# DESCRIPTION

virtio-net drives a virtio network device (Virtual I/O Device 1.2, section 5.1), as QEMU provides them, and serves it as an Ethernet device, ether(3).
devmgr(8) starts one for each device that matches its records (driver(6)):

```text
driver virtio-net
class net
match pci 1af4:1041
match pci 1af4:1000
```

The driver asks the device for its address, its link's state and its MTU, and for nothing else: no offloads, checksums or multiple queues.
A device with no address of its own gets `020000000001`; one with no MTU, 1500.
It keeps a receive queue full of 2 KiB buffers and sends from a transmit queue of as many, at most 128 each, which the device reads and writes through its own DMA: the driver refuses a device that does not let the IOMMU translate its addresses (a virtio device must offer `ACCESS_PLATFORM`; QEMU's does with `iommu_platform=on`).
The device's interrupt is MSI-X where devmgr gave the driver an MSI vector, else INTx, and with INTx the driver also polls the queues every 50 ms against a lost interrupt.
The link's state, which `ether` and `status` give, is read from the device when it may have changed: with INTx when the interrupt says its configuration did; with MSI-X, whose one vector also serves the queues and so can't say, at an interrupt at most every 500 ms, and 500 ms after the last read if an interrupt came meanwhile.
A change is seen within half a second.
A frame written when every transmit buffer is with the device is not sent, and counts as an output error.

# FILES

The driver's tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/virtio-net-<address>`.

: `ether`
  The device's ether(3) directory, which devmgr binds at `/net/etherN`.
: `status`
  Read only.
  `mac`, `mtu`, `link` (`up` or `down`), `features` (those in use, in hex) and `interrupt` (`MSI-X` or `INTx`), a line each; then the counts: `received <n> transmitted <n> dropped <n> unsent <n>` and `interrupts <n> polls <n>`, a poll being a wake without an interrupt: INTx's every 50 ms, or MSI-X's to read the link.
  `dropped` counts frames too short or too long; `unsent`, frames with no transmit buffer free.
  If setup failed, `FAILED: status <n>`, with a line before it saying why when the device bypasses the IOMMU.

`ether`'s `ifstats` reads as the counts' first line.

